                <tr id="issuerow12555108" rel="12555108" data-issuekey="CXF-4314" class="issuerow">
                                            <td class="project">    CXF
</td>
                                            <td class="issuekey">

    <a class="issue-link" data-issue-key="CXF-4314" href="https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/CXF-4314">CXF-4314</a>
</td>
                                            <td class="summary"><p>
                Introduce a search bean which can hold properties with different names
    </p>
</td>
                                            <td class="issuetype"> Improvement</td>
                                            <td class="status">
                Closed
    </td>
                                            <td class="priority">   Major
</td>
                                            <td class="resolution">    Fixed
</td>
                                            <td class="assignee">            Sergey Beryozkin
    </td>
                                            <td class="reporter">            Sergey Beryozkin
    </td>
                                            <td class="creator">            Sergey Beryozkin
    </td>
                                            <td class="created"> 14/May/12 09:38 </td>
                                            <td class="lastViewed"> &nbsp; </td>
                                            <td class="updated"> 05/Jun/12 13:31 </td>
                                            <td class="resolutiondate"> 14/May/12 10:11 </td>
                                            <td class="versions">    &nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="fixVersions">                        2.4.8,                     2.5.4,                     2.6.1            </td>
                                            <td class="components">                        JAX-RS            </td>
                                            <td class="duedate">    &nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="votes">0</td>
                                            <td class="watches">0</td>
                                            <td class="thumbnail"></td>
                                            <td class="timeoriginalestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timeestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timespent"></td>
                                            <td class="workratio">&nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment"></td>
                                            <td class="description">      At the moment, in order to capture a FIQL expression into a list of Search conditions, one needs to provide a bean which needs to have a matching property per every property name in the expression, for example, an expression such as &quot;name==bar;id=234&quot; will require a bean with properties &#39;name&#39; &amp; &#39;id&#39; available. This is reasonable when the set of properties is limited, but is too restricting in cases where one needs to experiment with an open-ended set of properties, which would require a bean to be updated every time a new search property gets introduced.<br/>
<br/>
Adding a simple SearchBean bean with a Map property will make it much simpler to convert FIQL expressions into SQL/etc expressions, as well as capture the FIQL expressions and analyze them without having to introduce a custom bean every time...<br/>
<br/>
For example, this is how it would look like when printing SQL expressions:<br/>
<br/>
{code:java}<br/>
// Search context represents this query: &quot;name==foo*;(name!=*bar,level=gt=10)&quot;<br/>
SearchCondition&lt;SearchBean&gt; filter = searchContext.getSearchCondition(SearchBean.class);<br/>
SQLPrinterVisitor&lt;SearchBean&gt; visitor = new SQLPrinterVisitor&lt;SearchBean&gt;(&quot;table&quot;);<br/>
filter.accept(visitor);<br/>
String sql = visitor.getResult();<br/>
assertTrue((&quot;SELECT * FROM table WHERE (name LIKE &#39;foo%&#39;) AND ((name NOT LIKE &#39;%bar&#39;) &quot;<br/>
                   + &quot;OR (level &gt; &#39;10&#39;))&quot;).equals(sql)<br/>
                   || (&quot;SELECT * FROM table WHERE (name LIKE &#39;foo%&#39;) AND &quot;<br/>
                   + &quot;((level &gt; &#39;10&#39;) OR (name NOT LIKE &#39;%bar&#39;))&quot;).equals(sql));<br/>
}<br/>
{code}<br/>

  </td>
